			<h1 id=3D"firstHeading" class=3D"firstHeading mw-first-heading"><span cl=
ass=3D"mw-page-title-main">NMI</span></h1>
			<div id=3D"bodyContent" class=3D"monobook-body">
				<div id=3D"siteSub">From NESdev Wiki</div>
				<div id=3D"contentSub"><div id=3D"mw-content-subtitle"></div></div>
			=09
				<div id=3D"jump-to-nav"></div><a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/NM=
I#column-one" class=3D"mw-jump-link">Jump to navigation</a><a href=3D"https=
://www.nesdev.org/wiki/NMI#searchInput" class=3D"mw-jump-link">Jump to sear=
ch</a>
				<!-- start content -->
				<div id=3D"mw-content-text" class=3D"mw-body-content"><div class=3D"mw-=
content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang=3D"en" dir=3D"ltr"><p>The <a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.nesdev.org/wiki/2A03" title=3D"2A03">2A03</a> and most other 6502 =
family CPUs are capable of processing a <b>non-maskable interrupt</b> (<b>N=
MI</b>). This calls a special NMI handler function after the current instru=
ction ends.
</p><p>The check for this is edge-sensitive, meaning that if other circuitr=
y on the board pulls the <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/CPU_pinout"=
 title=3D"CPU pinout">/NMI pin</a> from high to low voltage, this sets a fl=
ip-flop in the CPU.
When the CPU checks for interrupts and find that the flip-flop is set, it p=
ushes the current state onto the stack and jumps to the NMI handler address=
 at $FFFA-$FFFB.
</p><p>"Non-maskable" means that no state inside the CPU can prevent the NM=
I from being processed as an interrupt.
However, most boards that use a 6502 CPU's /NMI line allow the CPU to disab=
le the generation of /NMI signals by writing to a memory-mapped I/O device.
In the case of the NES, the /NMI line is connected to the NES PPU and is us=
ed to detect vertical blanking.
</p>
<div id=3D"toc" class=3D"toc" role=3D"navigation" aria-labelledby=3D"mw-toc=
-heading"><input type=3D"checkbox" role=3D"button" id=3D"toctogglecheckbox"=
 class=3D"toctogglecheckbox" style=3D"display:none" aria-label=3D"Hide the =
table of contents"><div class=3D"toctitle" lang=3D"en" dir=3D"ltr"><h2 id=
=3D"mw-toc-heading">Contents</h2><span class=3D"toctogglespan"><label class=
=3D"toctogglelabel" for=3D"toctogglecheckbox"></label></span></div>
<ul>
<li class=3D"toclevel-1 tocsection-1"><a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wik=
i/NMI#Operation"><span class=3D"tocnumber">1</span> <span class=3D"toctext"=
>Operation</span></a></li>
<li class=3D"toclevel-1 tocsection-2"><a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wik=
i/NMI#Caveats"><span class=3D"tocnumber">2</span> <span class=3D"toctext">C=
aveats</span></a>
<ul>
<li class=3D"toclevel-2 tocsection-3"><a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wik=
i/NMI#Old_emulators"><span class=3D"tocnumber">2.1</span> <span class=3D"to=
ctext">Old emulators</span></a></li>
<li class=3D"toclevel-2 tocsection-4"><a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wik=
i/NMI#Race_condition"><span class=3D"tocnumber">2.2</span> <span class=3D"t=
octext">Race condition</span></a></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Operation">Operation</h2></=
div>
<p>Two 1-bit registers inside the PPU control the generation of NMI signals=
.
<a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPU_frame_timing" title=3D"PPU frame=
 timing">Frame timing</a> and accesses to the PPU's <a href=3D"https://www.=
nesdev.org/wiki/PPUCTRL" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"PPUCTRL">PPUCTRL</a=
> and <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPUSTATUS" class=3D"mw-redirec=
t" title=3D"PPUSTATUS">PPUSTATUS</a> registers change these registers as fo=
llows, regardless of whether rendering is enabled:
</p>
<ol><li>Start of vertical blanking (scanline 241, dot 1): Set vblank_flag i=
n PPU to true.</li>
<li>End of vertical blanking (scanline 261, dot 1): Set vblank_flag to fals=
e.</li>
<li>Read <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPUSTATUS" class=3D"mw-redi=
rect" title=3D"PPUSTATUS">PPUSTATUS</a>: Return old status of vblank_flag i=
n bit 7, then set vblank_flag to false.</li>
<li>Write to <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPUCTRL" class=3D"mw-re=
direct" title=3D"PPUCTRL">PPUCTRL</a>: Set NMI_output to the state of bit 7=
.</li></ol>
<p>The PPU pulls /NMI low if and only if both vblank_flag and NMI_output ar=
e true.
By toggling NMI_output (<a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPUCTRL" cla=
ss=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"PPUCTRL">PPUCTRL</a>.7) during vertical blank w=
ithout reading <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPUSTATUS" class=3D"m=
w-redirect" title=3D"PPUSTATUS">PPUSTATUS</a>, a program can cause /NMI to =
be pulled low multiple times, causing multiple NMIs to be generated.
</p><p>When the CPU checks for an NMI and finds that the flip-flop is set, =
the CPU performs the following actions in order:
</p>
<ol><li>Push the return address high byte onto the stack.</li>
<li>Push the return address low byte onto the stack.</li>
<li>Push the processor status register onto the stack. Note that the <a hre=
f=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/Status_flags#The_B_flag" title=3D"Status f=
lags">B flag</a> will be set to 0.</li>
<li>Read the NMI handler address from $FFFA-$FFFB.</li>
<li>Clear the NMI handler flip-flop.</li>
<li>Set the program counter to the address read, jumping to the NMI handler=
.</li></ol>
<p>Generally, the NMI handler is expected to end with an <a href=3D"https:/=
/www.nesdev.org/wiki/Instruction_reference#RTI" title=3D"Instruction refere=
nce">RTI</a>. This returns the stack and status flags to their original sta=
te, continuing CPU operation as it was before the interrupt.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Caveats">Caveats</h2></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Old_emulators">Old emulator=
s</h3></div>
<p>Some platforms, such as the Game Boy, keep a flag turned on through the =
whole vertical blanking interval.
Some early emulators such as NESticle were developed under the assumption t=
hat <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPUSTATUS" class=3D"mw-redirect"=
 title=3D"PPUSTATUS">PPUSTATUS</a>.7 worked the same way and thus do <i>not=
</i> turn off vblank_flag in line 3.
Thus, some <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/Program_Compatibility" cl=
ass=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Program Compatibility">defective homebrew prog=
rams</a> developed in this era will wait for <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.=
org/wiki/PPUSTATUS" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"PPUSTATUS">PPUSTATUS</a>=
.7 to become false and expect this to happen at the end of vblank.
(The right way to wait for the end of vblank involves triggering a sprite 0=
 hit and waiting for <i>that</i> flag to become 0.)
Some newer homebrew programs have been known to display a diagnostic messag=
e if an emulator incorrectly returns true on two consecutive reads of <a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPUSTATUS" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D=
"PPUSTATUS">PPUSTATUS</a>.7.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Race_condition">Race condit=
ion</h3></div>
<p>If 1 and 3 happen simultaneously, <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki=
/PPUSTATUS" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"PPUSTATUS">PPUSTATUS</a> bit 7 i=
s read as false, and vblank_flag is set to false anyway. This means that th=
e NMI won't fire, and that the program will be unaware that the hardware is=
 in VBLANK.
This means that the following code that waits for vertical blank by spinnin=
g on <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPUSTATUS" class=3D"mw-redirect=
" title=3D"PPUSTATUS">PPUSTATUS</a> bit 7 is likely to miss an occasional f=
rame:
</p>
<pre>wait_status7:
    bit PPUSTATUS
    bpl wait_status7
    rts
</pre>
<p>Code like <code>wait_status7</code> is fine while your program is waitin=
g for <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/PPU_power_up_state" title=3D"P=
PU power up state">the PPU to warm up</a>.
But once the game is running, the most reliable way to wait for a vertical =
blank is to turn on NMI_output and then wait for the NMI handler to set a v=
ariable:
</p>
<pre>wait_nmi:
    lda retraces
@notYet:
    cmp retraces
    beq @notYet
    rts

nmi_handler:
    inc retraces
    rti
</pre>
<p>This handler works for simple cases, and in many cases, it is <a rel=3D"=
nofollow" class=3D"external text" href=3D"http://c2.com/xp/DoTheSimplestThi=
ngThatCouldPossiblyWork.html">the simplest thing that could possibly work</=
a>.
But if your game code takes significantly longer than 24,000 cycles, such a=
s if you have too many critters moving on the screen, it may take longer th=
an one frame.
Waiting for NMI in this way would miss an NMI that happens while other code=
 is running.
In some cases, this could cause a sprite 0-triggered scroll split to flicke=
r (or worse).
The next step up involves doing VRAM updates and sprite 0 waiting in a sepa=
rate <a href=3D"https://www.nesdev.org/wiki/NMI_thread" title=3D"NMI thread=
">NMI thread</a> that is guaranteed to run every frame.
</p><p>Because Gradius puts its status bar at the bottom, it can't just spi=
n on sprite 0 all the time.
Instead, it counts the approximate time that each object handler takes and =
deliberately delays the remaining calculations to the next frame when it mi=
ght otherwise come close to missing a sprite 0 hit.
Games developed by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micronics" clas=
s=3D"extiw" title=3D"wikipedia:Micronics">Micronics</a> are likely to reduc=
e a game's overall frame rate far below 60 frames per second to match the w=
orst case of lag.
</p>
